<title>USA: Most LME aluminum &quot;tied up&quot; for long-term - Spector.</title>
<headline>Most LME aluminum &quot;tied up&quot; for long-term - Spector.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1996-09-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Most of the rise in London Metal Exchange primary aluminum inventories has been ingot moving into warehouses under long-term storage arrangements lasting six months to a year, industry analyst Stewart Spector said.</p>
<p>&quot;Metal stored under these deals now accounts for over 72 percent of LME warehouse stocks,&quot; Spector writes in his Aluminum Industry Service.</p>
<p>LME primary aluminum stocks rose 75 tonnes to 947,700 tonnes Friday.</p>
